<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · Sacred · c. 570 BCE · Ancient Near East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Psalm 137</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="hbo" dir="rtl">Super flumina Babylonis</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song from the Bible sung by people taken far from home to Babylon, who missed their city of Jerusalem.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous (attributed by rabbinical sources to the prophet Jeremiah)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>The people of Judah in Babylonian exile; later Jewish and Christian worship</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Biblical Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Babylonia, present-day Iraq</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>during or after the Babylonian exile (deportations from 597 BCE; Babylon fell in 539 BCE)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament · Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; a psalm of the Hebrew Bible from the time of the Babylonian exile)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>After Nebuchadnezzar II of Babylon took Jerusalem in 597 BC, and in the campaigns that followed, people of the Kingdom of Judah were deported to Babylonia, where they were held until some time after the fall of Babylon in 539 BC. Psalm 137 gives voice to that exile. It is a communal lament of nine verses, set by the rivers of Babylon, the Euphrates, its tributaries and the Tigris, in which the exiles mourn Jerusalem, refuse their captors' demand for a song, vow never to forget the city, and end with a call for violent revenge on its enemies. Rabbinical sources attributed it to the prophet Jeremiah, and the Septuagint heads it &quot;For David. By Jeremias, in the Captivity.&quot; The Greek and Latin Bibles number it Psalm 136; in Latin it is known by its opening words, &quot;Super flumina Babylonis&quot;. The Methodist writer Joseph Benson read the harps of the psalm as standing for all instruments of music and its singers as probably Levites, the musicians of the Temple's service.</p><p>The psalm has a fixed place in Jewish practice. It is recited before the Grace After Meals on weekdays (Psalm 126 replaces it on the Sabbath, holidays and festive meals) and before Tisha B'Av, the fast that mourns the destruction of the Temples in Jerusalem. Verses 5 and 6 are said at Jewish weddings just before a glass is broken in mourning for the Temple, and it is one of the ten psalms of the Tikkun HaKlali of Rebbe Nachman of Breslov.</p><p>Christian churches have prayed it for centuries: at Matins in the Eastern Orthodox Church, solemnly on the Sundays before Lent, in the Coptic book of hours, and at Wednesday Vespers in the Roman Breviary following the Rule of Saint Benedict. Its last verses have long troubled its users. After the Second Vatican Council the last three verses were removed from Catholic liturgical books as incompatible with the Gospel message, the 1962 Book of Common Prayer of the Anglican Church of Canada removed them too, and many musical settings leave out the final verse.</p><p>Composers have set it in every period: Latin motets by Palestrina, Lassus and Victoria; a German rhymed paraphrase by Wolfgang Dachstein, first published in 1525, which became a Lutheran hymn and was also taken up in Ashkenazi Jewish culture; Salamone Rossi's Hebrew setting; settings by Schütz and an organ chorale prelude by Bach. In America William Billings turned it into a lament for Boston under British occupation. Lord Byron paraphrased it in his Hebrew Melodies in 1815, and it inspired the chorus of Hebrew slaves, &quot;Va, pensiero&quot;, in Verdi's opera Nabucco (1842). In 1970 Brent Dowe and Trevor McNaughton of the Jamaican reggae group the Melodians based the Rastafarian song &quot;Rivers of Babylon&quot; partly on its opening verses; Boney M.'s version of 1978 made it widely known.</p><p>The psalm has also been carried into politics and protest. Pope Gregory X quoted it when he left the Crusade upon his election in the papal conclave of 1268–1271. Frederick Douglass, in his speech &quot;What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?&quot;, compared being asked to speak on the Fourth of July to the exiles of the psalm being asked to sing in a foreign land.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A lament of exiles far from their homeland: they grieve for Jerusalem, will not make music for those who hold them captive, and bind themselves never to forget the city. Its mood moves from quiet mourning to an oath of loyalty and, at the end, to a bitter curse calling for revenge on the city's enemies, including violence against their children.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Biblical Hebrew</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Biblical Hebrew</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">עַ֥ל־נַהֲר֨וֹת ׀ בָּבֶ֗ל</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">al-naharot bavel</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>By the rivers of Babylon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שָׁ֣ם יָ֭שַׁבְנוּ גַּם־בָּכִ֑ינוּ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">sham yashavnu gam-bakhinu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>there we sat, and we also wept,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">בְּ֝זׇכְרֵ֗נוּ אֶת־צִיּֽוֹן׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">bezokhrenu et-tsiyyon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>when we remembered Zion.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">עַֽל־עֲרָבִ֥ים בְּתוֹכָ֑הּ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">al-aravim betokhah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On the willows [?] in its midst</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">תָּ֝לִ֗ינוּ כִּנֹּרוֹתֵֽינוּ׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">talinu kinnorotenu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we hung up our lyres.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">כִּ֤י שָׁ֨ם שְֽׁאֵל֪וּנוּ שׁוֹבֵ֡ינוּ דִּבְרֵי־שִׁ֭יר</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">ki sham sheelunu shovenu divre-shir</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For there our captors asked us for words of song,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">וְתוֹלָלֵ֣ינוּ שִׂמְחָ֑ה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">vetolalenu simkhah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and our tormentors [?] for joy:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שִׁ֥ירוּ לָ֗֝נוּ מִשִּׁ֥יר צִיּֽוֹן׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">shiru lanu mishshir tsiyyon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Sing us one of the songs of Zion.&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אֵ֗יךְ נָשִׁ֥יר אֶת־שִׁיר־יְהֹוָ֑ה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">ekh nashir et-shir-YHWH</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>How shall we sing the song of YHWH</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">עַ֗֝ל אַדְמַ֥ת נֵכָֽר׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">al admat nekhar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>on foreign soil?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אִֽם־אֶשְׁכָּחֵ֥ךְ יְֽרוּשָׁלָ֗͏ִם</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">im-eshkakhekh yerushalayim</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If I forget you, Jerusalem,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">תִּשְׁכַּ֥ח יְמִינִֽי׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">tishkakh yemini</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let my right hand forget.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">תִּדְבַּֽק־לְשׁוֹנִ֨י ׀ לְחִכִּי֮</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">tidbaq-leshoni lekhikki</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let my tongue cling to my palate</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אִם־לֹ֢א אֶ֫זְכְּרֵ֥כִי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">im-lo ezkerekhi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if I do not remember you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אִם־לֹ֣א אַ֭עֲלֶה אֶת־יְרוּשָׁלַ֑͏ִם</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">im-lo aaleh et-yerushalayim</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if I do not raise Jerusalem</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">עַ֗֝ל רֹ֣אשׁ שִׂמְחָתִֽי׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">al rosh simkhati</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>above the head of my joy.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">זְכֹ֤ר יְהֹוָ֨ה ׀ לִבְנֵ֬י אֱד֗וֹם</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">zekhor YHWH livne edom</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Remember, YHWH, against the sons of Edom</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אֵת֮ י֤וֹם יְֽר֫וּשָׁלָ֥͏ִם</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">et yom yerushalayim</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the day of Jerusalem,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">הָ֭אֹ֣מְרִים עָ֤רוּ ׀ עָ֑רוּ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">haomerim aru aru</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>those who said, &quot;Lay it bare, lay it bare,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">עַ֗֝ד הַיְס֥וֹד בָּֽהּ׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">ad hayesod bah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>down to its foundation!&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">בַּת־בָּבֶ֗ל הַשְּׁד֫וּדָ֥ה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">bat-bavel hashshedudah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Daughter of Babylon, you who are laid waste,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אַשְׁרֵ֥י שֶׁיְשַׁלֶּם־לָ֑ךְ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">ashre sheyeshallem-lakh</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>happy is he who repays you</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אֶת־גְּ֝מוּלֵ֗ךְ שֶׁגָּמַ֥לְתְּ לָֽנוּ׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">et-gemulekh shegamalt lanu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the deed that you dealt to us.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אַשְׁרֵ֤י ׀ שֶׁיֹּאחֵ֓ז וְנִפֵּ֬ץ אֶֽת־עֹלָלַ֗יִךְ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">ashre sheyyokhez venippets et-olalayikh</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Happy is he who seizes and dashes your little ones</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אֶל־הַסָּֽלַע׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">el-hassala</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>against the rock.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Psalm 137, in the Masoretic text. Text from <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%AA%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%99%D7%9D_%D7%A7%D7%9C%D7%96" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (SBL general-purpose).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>The divine name is written יְהֹוָה (the four letters YHWH with the vowels of Adonai, &quot;Lord&quot;); it is romanized and translated as YHWH. In reading it is said &quot;Adonai&quot;.</li><li>Cantillation marks, the paseq (׀) and sof pasuq (׃) are not romanized; maqaf is shown as a hyphen.</li><li>line 2.1: 'aravim' is traditionally 'willows'; most botanists take it as the Euphrates poplar.</li><li>line 3.2: 'tolalenu' occurs only here; 'our tormentors' (or 'our plunderers') is the usual reading, but the sense is uncertain.</li><li>line 5.2: the Hebrew has no object: 'let my right hand forget [its skill]' or 'wither' (a common emendation) are both proposed.</li><li>line 7.3: 'aru' means 'strip bare, raze'.</li><li>line 8.1: 'hashshedudah' is 'the devastated one'; many read it as 'who is to be devastated'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="HNBU-W0UmUU" data-title="Psalm 137: The Marian Consort, Palestrina's setting &quot;Super flumina Babylonis&quot;"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">The Marian Consort, Palestrina's setting &quot;Super flumina Babylonis&quot;</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The Marian Consort. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HNBU-W0UmUU" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Super flumina Babylonis, motets (Costanzo Festa, Nicolas Gombert, Palestrina, Orlando di Lasso, Philippe de Monte, Tomás Luis de Victoria)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">16th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">An Wasserflüssen Babylon (Wolfgang Dachstein)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1525</span><span class="sg-chain-h">German rhymed paraphrase and setting, adopted as a Lutheran hymn</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sôbolos rios que vão por Babilónia (Luís de Camões)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">16th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">poem based on the psalm</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Le long des eaux, ou se bagne (Eustache Du Caurroy)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1610</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting of Gilles Durant de la Bergerie's paraphrase</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Al naharot Bavel (Salamone Rossi)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Hebrew setting for four voices</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">An den Wassern zu Babel, SWV 37 (Heinrich Schütz)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1619</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting of Luther's translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">An Wasserflüssen Babylon, chorale prelude (Johann Sebastian Bach)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">organ work on Dachstein's hymn</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Super flumina Babylonis, H.170 (Marc-Antoine Charpentier)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1670</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pamela; or, Virtue Rewarded (Samuel Richardson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1740</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted in the novel</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lamentation over Boston (William Billings)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted to the British occupation of Boston</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Na rekakh Vavilonskikh (Artemy Vedel)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">two choral concertos in Ukrainian</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hebrew Melodies (Lord Byron)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1815</span><span class="sg-chain-h">versified paraphrase</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hebräische Gesänge, Op. 4 No. 2 (Carl Loewe)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1823</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting of Franz Theremin's German translation of Byron</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Va, pensiero, in Nabucco (Giuseppe Verdi)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1842</span><span class="sg-chain-h">inspired by the psalm</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Super flumina Babylonis: Paraphrase, Op. 52 (Charles-Valentin Alkan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1859</span><span class="sg-chain-h">piano piece</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Près du fleuve étranger (Charles Gounod)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1861</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting of a French paraphrase</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Super Flumina Babylonis (Gabriel Fauré)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1863</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting for chorus and orchestra</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Biblical Songs No. 7 (Antonín Dvořák)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1894</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting of verses 1–5</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Belshazzar's Feast (William Walton)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1931</span><span class="sg-chain-h">opening section set as a song of the captives</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">If I Forget Thee, Jerusalem (William Faulkner)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1939</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title of the novel</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Two Psalms (Harry Partch)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1942</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Rivers of Babylon (The Melodians)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1970</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Rastafarian song based partly on the opening verses</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">On the Willows, in Godspell (Stephen Schwartz)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1971</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Babylon (Don McLean)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1971</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover of Philip Hayes's round on the first two verses</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">An den Wassern zu Babel saßen wir und weinten (Arvo Pärt)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1976</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Rivers of Babylon (Boney M.)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1978</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover of the Melodians' song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">In Exile (Herbert Sumsion)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1981</span><span class="sg-chain-h">motet on verses 1–6</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">By the Rivers Dark (Leonard Cohen)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2001</span><span class="sg-chain-h">inspired by the psalm</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jerusalem (Matisyahu)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2006</span><span class="sg-chain-h">chorus based on verses 5–6</span></li><li><a href="/songs/jerusalem-of-gold">Jerusalem of Gold</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1967</span><span class="sg-chain-h">line drawn from</span></li><li><a href="/songs/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero (Chorus of the Hebrew Slaves)</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1842</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the libretto was inspired by it</span></li></ul></section>
